i accidentally became a meme and this is  that story  i'm tazonday i am probably best known  for the viral video  chocolate ring and have also done a lot  of other stuff  i was in graduate school on a career  path to become  a historian i made music as a hobby  while i lived in minneapolis i got tired  of dragging equipment to open mics in  the middle of the winter in minneapolis  where it's very cold  youtube came along and i said hey why  don't i  start filming music in my living room  and putting it on this youtube thing  and it ended up being bigger than i  expected  the riff had been a riff of mine for  many years  the lyrics i wrote over a period of  about six weeks  as a serious ballad about racism in some  ways perhaps it's a literal metaphor for  me having brown skin and also the way  that i play the riff on the piano being  a little bit rain  like i uploaded chocolate rain to  youtube as an afterthought  it wasn't intended as the final version  it was just an experimental  ballad and poem it did not blow up  immediately someone  eventually posted it on digg.com someone  saw it there they posted it on 4chan  i would say it became viral largely in  part  because i had a voice body mismatch you  know i  look like bruno mars i sound like barry  white i move like mr bean  and a lot of 4chan's audience saw me as  a potential patron  saint as sort of like oh this is a nerd  he's  totally not self-aware and he's being  big and loud  and this is like perfect and it  started to go massively viral i did the  worst satellite interview ever on cnn i  had no  experience in public relations it's an  awkward nerd plucked out of his living  room  and everybody three of the four major  labels of the time  i want to sign me as an artist everyone  is contacting me to sing at their kids  bar mitzvah at their corporate party  i just kind of said yes and i didn't  know what yes  meant necessarily the dr pepper video  was a fun video  some people criticize it in some ways  everybody did that you know michael  jackson used his songs and major  sponsorships  loud all richie and whatnot so i did  have a little bit of a precedent for  doing that type of pivot with the song  people who work at ad agencies they'll  tell me  that video was in a case study that i  read in college and i'm like  oh i didn't know that was in a marketing  case study i have been  very lucky to pursue a career in  entertainment  both acting voice work narration and  other things that stemmed from that  viral moment of chocolate rain  why wasn't the world ready in 2007 for  a serious message about racism that  perhaps it was  ready for in 2020. in 2007  youtube was not considered mainstream  new things would become viral  in a novelty economy around 2012  recommendation engines started to show  people what they wanted to see on the  internet  it sort of supercharged the ability of  people to fall off onto their own  islands  of their own prejudices and biases  and live in a reality distortion field  so there was a sort of lag time where  anti-racism and progressive thought  had to mobilize itself against  that onslaught what gives me hope is  that a 12-year-old who is watching  chocolate rain today  has many more tools to understand the  actual message of a critique of  institutional racism  than a 12 year old who was watching it  in 2007.  [Music]  [Music]  you
 

